{% doc %}
  Sends the customer a receipt saying their email arrived and became a
  ticket.

  Goes out through the ticket's own email provider rather than through
  platformOS, unlike the @mention email, because this one has to thread.
  It carries a Message-ID, In-Reply-To and the [#number] token, so when
  the customer replies to the receipt the reply lands on the ticket
  instead of opening a second one.

  It is not an answer, and nothing here pretends otherwise: the ticket is
  not touched, so it still says the customer is waiting, and
  first_replied_at stays empty until a person writes something.

  Refuses in three cases, each returning a reason rather than failing:
  a department with no from address has nowhere to send from, a contact
  acknowledged in the last day does not need another, and a ticket that
  already has an outbound message has been answered by a human faster
  than this job ran.

  @param {object} ticket - The new ticket
  @param {object} department - Its department
  @param {object} contact - The customer
  @param {object} settings - The settings record
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  if department.from_address == blank
    comment
      Nothing to send from. A half configured install should go quiet
      rather than send mail from an address nobody has verified.
    endcomment
    assign result = { "sent": false, "reason": "no_from_address" }
    return result
  endif

  assign now = 'now' | date: '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'

  comment
    One receipt per address per day. Two machines acknowledging each
    other is the loop this whole guard exists to prevent, and the
    cheapest place to break it is here, on the second one.
  endcomment
  if contact.last_acknowledged_at != blank
    assign last = contact.last_acknowledged_at | date: '%s' | plus: 0
    assign cutoff = 'now' | date: '%s' | minus: 86400
    if last > cutoff
      assign result = { "sent": false, "reason": "recently_acknowledged" }
      return result
    endif
  endif

  function messages = 'modules/support/queries/messages/for_ticket', ticket_id: ticket.id
  for message in messages
    if message.kind == 'outbound'
      assign result = { "sent": false, "reason": "already_answered" }
      return result
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    Claim the slot before writing or sending anything. This runs as a
    background job with retries, and a retry that sends a second receipt
    to a customer is worse than a retry that does nothing.
  endcomment
  graphql _ = 'modules/support/contacts/set_acknowledged', id: contact.id, last_acknowledged_at: now

  assign body = 'modules/support/emails.acknowledgement.body' | t: number: ticket.number
  function email = 'modules/support/helpers/email/build_reply', ticket: ticket, department: department, contact: contact, messages: messages, body: body, cc: null

  assign recipients = contact.email | split: ','
  assign outbound = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "kind": "outbound", "user_id": null, "from_address": email.from_address, "to_addresses": recipients, "cc_addresses": null, "subject": email.subject, "body_text": email.body, "message_id_header": email.message_id, "in_reply_to": email.in_reply_to, "references": email.references, "delivery_status": "sending" }

  function message = 'modules/support/commands/messages/create', object: outbound
  unless message.valid
    log message.errors, type: 'ERROR: modules/support/commands/notifications/acknowledge could not record the receipt'
    assign result = { "sent": false, "reason": "not_recorded" }
    return result
  endunless

  function delivery = 'modules/support/commands/outbound/send', object: email, settings: settings
  graphql _ = 'modules/support/messages/set_delivery', id: message.id, delivery_status: delivery.status, provider_message_id: delivery.provider_message_id

  assign result = { "sent": delivery.sent, "reason": delivery.status, "message_id": message.id }
  return result
%}
